In our color-organized society the primary moral facts are the warm or cool hues that persons and acts inherently embody and the obligations those hues generate to preserve chromatic balance. Mocking a vulnerable (typically cool-hued) group is itself a sharply warm, clashing act whose hue is fixed by its content; the absence of the targeted people does not bleach the act’s color, nor does the private setting, because color radiates regardless of witnesses. One friend’s later discomfort is simply the cool residue of having briefly resonated with that dissonant warmth, confirming that the act disturbed the proper order of hues.  

Thus the considerations that matter are those that track intrinsic color and its social diffusion, not mere privacy or harm-as-consequence.

